\details{
Pearson residuals are the raw residuals divided by the square root of the fitted marginal variance. They rely only on the marginal mean and variance models, i.e., the same assumptions made by SAGEE itself.

Dunn-Smyth residuals (Dunn and Smyth, 1996) are computed as \eqn{\Phi^{-1}(u)}, where \eqn{\Phi} denotes the standard normal distribution function, and \eqn{u = F(y)} where \eqn{F} is the fitted marginal distribution function. For discrete responses, some "jittering" is added by drawing \eqn{u} uniformly between \eqn{F(y^-)} (the CDF at the lower limit approaching from the left) and \eqn{F(y)}. If the fitted marginal distribution is correct, the Dunn-Smyth residuals are exactly standard normal, which makes them much better suited to normal probability plots than Pearson residuals for discrete data. On the other hand, they come with a big caveat as follows: since SAGEE is a generalized estimating equation approach, meaning it only assumes a model for the marginal mean and the marginal variance of the responses (along with a marginal working correlation structure across sites), then \strong{it does not assume a full, explicit distribution for the actual response}. Dunn-Smyth residuals however require a full marginal distribution. Therefore, a compromise is made in this function by \strong{choosing a distribution for the response that arises as the "natural" choice implied by each family's mean and mean-variance relationship}; see \code{\link[mvabund:residuals.manyglm]{mvabund::residuals.manyglm()}} for an analogous assumption.

Put simply, Dunn-Smyth residuals assess an additional assumption that SAGEE itself does not make. This means a poor-looking normal probability plot of Dunn-Smyth residuals, say, may therefore reflect an incorrect (but superfluous) distributional assumption, even when the mean and variance models, and hence the SAGEE estimates, are perfectly adequate. Pearson residuals remain the more direct check of the assumptions that SAGEE actually makes, and Dunn-Smyth residuals are best treated as a complementary, somewhat stricter, residual choice.
}
